  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 259 AM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nThursday through Saturday Night...\n\nFrom Thursday through the first half of the weekend, the synoptic \npattern should feature ridging across the western CONUS and off the \neast coast, with broad, flat troughing extending across the Midwest. \nThis should allow a sfc cold front to approach our area from the \nnorthwest Thursday; however, it is currently not expected to move \nthrough the area as the upper trough will lack sufficient amplitude. \nDiurnal shower/storm chances should increase as we head later into \nthe week and into the weekend, with LREF mean PWAT values rising to \n1.75-1.9\" and SBCAPE values rising to 1500-2000 J/kg. While this \nshould be sufficient for at least isolated to scattered rain \nchances, some medium-range progs (e.g., 0Z ECMWF) depict a couple \nmid-level shortwaves passing across the region Friday into Saturday. \nIf these shortwaves pass during peak heating, greater concentration \nof showers and storms would be expected, though confidence in timing \nis still fairly low. \n\nIt still doesn't look like the potential for strong to severe storms \nwill be all that great, with LREF mean bulk shear values of 15-20 kt \nmore supportive of garden-variety convection. As the previous \ndiscussion mentioned, deep warm cloud (>= 0C) depths and seasonably \nhigh PWATs could support a few instances of heavy rain/flash \nflooding Friday into Saturday, as highlighted by the WPC Excessive \nRainfall Outlook. Temperatures during the late week/early weekend \nshould be fairly seasonable, with highs ranging from the mid 80s to \nthe low 90s.\n\nSunday into Early Next Week...\n\nAs the previous discussion outlined, much of the forecast for late \nin the weekend through early-to-mid next week will depend on the \ninterplay between western US ridging and troughing over Hudson Bay. \nWPC 500 mb height clusters show that a more expansive ridge and more \nzonal flow aloft would support a generally warmer and drier pattern \nas we head into early next week. In contrast, a more amplified \npattern would bring the storm track closer to the Ohio Valley early \nnext week, leading to generally cooler and stormier conditions. At \nthis time, the forecast tends toward the former solution, though \nwe'll continue to monitor trends over coming days.",
